<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kachchhapaghata dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Gopadri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rajput dynasty that held the fortress of Gwalior and built its Sas-Bahu temple in the 11th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kachchhapaghatas emerged in the 10th century in the region of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narwar">Narwar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubkund">Dubkund</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, probably as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a>. According to a Gwalior inscription, Vajradaman took the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gopadri</a>, Gwalior, from the Pratiharas around 977.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Several branches of the family ruled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narwar">Narwar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubkund">Dubkund</a>. They were often <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Chandelas and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paramara-dynasty">Paramara dynasty</a>; Kirttiraja is said to have defeated an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. Mahipala completed the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sas-bahu-temple">Sas-Bahu</a> temple at Gwalior fort in 1093, and the Dubkund branch built Jain temples, showing patronage of both faiths.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 12th century the Kachchhapaghatas lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> to a Pratihara family, and the region was later taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in 1232. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha Rajputs</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaipur-state">Amber</a> claimed descent from them.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kachchhapaghata-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kachchhapaghata dynasty" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Airunp assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sas_Bahu_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gopadri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vajradaman, Kirttiraja, Mahipala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kachchhapaghatas, Kacchapaghatas of Gwalior</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1050&amp;focus=kachchhapaghata-dynasty" aria-label="Kachchhapaghata dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6776 1332 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kachchhapaghata dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7121 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7096 1588h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7096 1588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=1050&amp;focus=kachchhapaghata-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

